A||||7||fiero56||fuel guage sending unit||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||10-20-2000||12:51 AM||||I just purchased an '86SE v-6 4-speed last week. Having my Formula for over a year, I know the poblem of the fuel guage giving a false reading(too high). I haven't really driven this car yet, so I don't(didn't)know how the guage was working. Since the engine wouldn't run, and there was no fuel pressure, I figured it was the fuel pump. I bought a new one, and installed it this evening. When I pulled the tank, it was empty, but the guage showed just a shade over 1/2 full. I replaced the pump, played with all that fun stuff, ran to the gas station, grabbed 5 gallons of gas, and put it in the SE. Now the guage reads WAY over the "full" mark, it is just over the horizontal axis, and I'm sure if I actually fill the tank, it will read higher. <P>Any way I can fix this without taking the tank out again?<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pffsig1.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||RossT||10-20-2000||12:58 AM||||Sound like the sending unit to me.  Read Ray Paulks article on this.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.mn.org/Knowledge/Procedure.asp?Version=w6x2v73u&Catid=22&Id=46&Action=Edit" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.mn.org/Knowledge/Procedure.asp?Version=w6x2v73u&Catid=22&Id=46&Action=Edit</A> <P>Most people are just pulling the needle of and replacing it to show the actual fuel level.<P>Ross||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||10-20-2000||01:08 AM||||Yanking the needle off appeals much more to me than pulling that tank out again, even though if I were my usual meticulous self, I would be out there, pulling the tank again.<P>So, it is possible to just yank off the needle, and stick it on the "full" hash mark after I fill the tank at the station?||||reg||1
Z||000003||RossT||10-20-2000||01:19 AM||||My new gt is doing the same thing.  Reads about 1/4-1/3 to much.  I am in the process of running a cleaner thru the gas to see if that helps, (the car sat in storage for 10 years) if not, I will be doing the needle thing.  I know that many on the forum have done it. You might need (not sure) to use a small amount of glue when you put the needle back on.  If I were you I would do that, rather than pulling the tank again to replace the sending unit.||||reg||1
Z||000004||redfiero||10-20-2000||06:45 AM||||Drew,<P>I reset the needle.  I wouldn't advise that if you can help it.  When my car is full, the needle reads correctly on full.  However, I run out of gas at about a 1/4 tank.  Its probably well worth replacing the sending unit.<P>matt||||reg||1
Z||000005||sparks||10-20-2000||07:52 AM||||Sounds familiar.  I just ran out of gas yesterday afternoon because I didn't know about this problem!  It was registering 1/4 tank of gas and quit on my 4 miles from home!<P><P>------------------<BR>--<BR>sparks||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||10-20-2000||08:56 AM||||My gauge isn't too bad but I've learned not to trust it with the small fuel tank on the Fieros.  I always reset my trip odometer when I refill and use it as a back-up to know when I've gone far enough.  I don't like to get below half tank if I can help it unless I'm strictly on a road trip.  Commuting to work I try to keep it above half because I never know when I'll get stuck in traffic.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Raydar||10-20-2000||11:39 PM||||I had a temp gauge that would slowly "go ballistic" as the engine warmed up. Actual engine temp was pretty normal. <BR>Turned out to be a bad connection where the ceramic resistor is fastened to the pins on the back of the gauge. I removed the tin nuts, carefully scraped the tarnish off of the silver plating on the ceramic, and put the nuts back on. <BR>I don't remember if the gas gauge has this same ceramic resistor or not. May be worth a look.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 10-20-2000).]||||reg||1
